1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,940
Over the years, attitudes towards workers have changed considerably.

2
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:13,500
After all, there was a time when workers had no rights at all

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:17,150
and laboured in appalling conditions.

4
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:24,170
Conditions have improved a lot, but conflict in the workplace is still common.

5
00:00:24,170 --> 00:00:32,270
And human resources managers nowadays need to be able to deal with it when necessary.

6
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:36,033
What is conflict in the workplace?

7
00:00:36,033 --> 00:00:47,950
Definitions vary, but I'm taking it to refer to a whole range of behaviours that the victim finds unacceptable,

8
00:00:47,950 --> 00:00:54,850
from minor, harmless arguments to - at the opposite extreme - physical violence.

9
00:00:54,850 --> 00:01:00,291
Much of this is covered by the term bullying, by

10
00:01:00,291 --> 00:01:07,900
which I mean one or more people behaving abusively or aggressively against another

11
00:01:07,900 --> 00:01:10,630
who is in a weaker position.

12
00:01:10,630 --> 00:01:15,300
Although all behaviour like this is a form of conflict

13
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:20,120
not all conflict can be described in these terms.

14
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,208
As with all human behaviour, there are numerous reasons for it.

15
00:01:25,208 --> 00:01:28,800
But often it's caused by someone

16
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:33,071
who feels the need to show their superiority over someone else,

17
00:01:33,071 --> 00:01:41,860
in order to feel that they aren't at the lowest level in a hierarchy or a group of people.

18
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:46,595
In some cases one person simply dislikes the other,

19
00:01:46,595 --> 00:01:56,590
on the basis that the personality of one is in some way incompatible with that of the other person.

20
00:01:56,590 --> 00:02:04,862
A general habit of optimism in one person could make them intolerant of a colleague

21
00:02:04,862 --> 00:02:11,480
who's constantly pessimistic - not that that justifies treating them badly, of course.

22
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:17,326
Some conflicts arise when people are more interested in promoting themselves

23
00:02:17,326 --> 00:02:22,640
and their team than in the company as a whole.

24
00:02:22,640 --> 00:02:27,608
These conflicts are called 'structural', and could come about,

25
00:02:27,608 --> 00:02:34,500
for example, when a sales team believe they are the only people in the business

26
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:38,648
who do any useful work,

27
00:02:38,648 --> 00:02:41,960
and look down on behind. The-scenes administrators.

28
00:02:41,960 --> 00:02:45,838
Conflict obviously affects the individuals concerned -

29
00:02:45,838 --> 00:02:52,302
the situation is likely to be very stressful for victims,

30
00:02:52,302 --> 00:02:58,120
resulting in their absence from work, possibly for months.

31
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:03,618
For the company, if no effort is made to deal with conflict,

32
00:03:03,618 --> 00:03:10,490
it can spiral out of control, and even lead to the breakdown of the business.

33
00:03:10,490 --> 00:03:14,204
Some interesting work with chief executives -

34
00:03:14,204 --> 00:03:22,250
CEOs - has uncovered some of the reasons why they may treat colleagues badly.

35
00:03:22,250 --> 00:03:25,995
Many CEOs combine two opposing characteristics:

36
00:03:25,995 --> 00:03:34,940
confidence - that is, the belief that they're capable of great achievements

37
00:03:35,480 --> 00:03:36,604
with a high level of anxiety,

38
00:03:36,604 --> 00:03:41,020
a fear of missing targets

39
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:46,590
whether set by themselves or by the directors of the company.

40
00:03:46,590 --> 00:03:54,530
This combination can make them respond badly to anyone who questions their decisions.

41
00:03:54,530 --> 00:04:01,500
In a high pressure work environment, such characteristics become problematic.

42
00:04:01,500 --> 00:04:06,783
And it's particularly difficult to tackle the situation where colleagues,

43
00:04:06,783 --> 00:04:13,122
managers and board members are all trying to achieve their own visions.

44
00:04:13,122 --> 00:04:18,800
When they can't agree on strategic issues and on

45
00:04:18,800 --> 00:04:23,160
where they see the business going, there are real problems.

46
00:04:23,160 --> 00:04:27,400
For managers at lower levels within the organisation

47
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:32,107
it might seem that an autocratic form of management -

48
00:04:32,107 --> 00:04:37,940
where the chief executive gives orders and everyone else has to obey

49
00:04:38,520 --> 00:04:41,580
would see more conflict than others.

50
00:04:41,580 --> 00:04:52,607
Interestingly, though, a company with a more democratic business model can suffer more when uncertainty about

51
00:04:52,607 --> 00:04:58,120
who to report to leads to conflicting demands.

52
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:06,350
Now I'll say a little about dealing with the type of conflict that has harmful effects.

53
00:05:06,350 --> 00:05:12,614
Of course the ideal is to prevent it arising in the first place.

54
00:05:12,614 --> 00:05:15,505
A good manager, at any level,

55
00:05:15,505 --> 00:05:20,770
will make efforts to earn the respect of the people they work with

56
00:05:21,680 --> 00:05:24,660
particularly those who report to them.

57
00:05:24,660 --> 00:05:28,287
That will involve politeness in all communications,

58
00:05:28,287 --> 00:05:31,560
and treating them as equals

59
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:36,060
who happen to have a different role within the organisation.

60
00:05:36,060 --> 00:05:43,205
Sometimes, of course, conflict does occur, and can get out of hand.

61
00:05:43,205 --> 00:05:49,160
In such cases the human resources department often gets involved.

62
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:59,774
However, if one of the parties in a conflict sees human resources as simply a mouthpiece for the chief executive,

63
00:05:59,774 --> 00:06:04,550
then an external mediator might be able to help.

64
00:06:04,550 --> 00:06:07,393
By talking to both sides,

65
00:06:07,393 --> 00:06:11,180
and trying to find the truth of what's been happening

66
00:06:12,040 --> 00:06:18,197
they can build a clear picture of the situation,

67
00:06:18,197 --> 00:06:25,020
and give feedback that both sides will accept, precisely because they're independent.
